.0, "environment": 7.11, "incumbency": 3.0, "money": 1.45}, "rating_margin": 8.25, "sigma_race": 5.5, "sigma_total": 6.3, "baseline_margin": 8.78, "analyst_adjustment": -1.78, "key_factors": ["Ossoff's massive cash advantage ($42.6M vs $2.1M) and incumbency", "Consistent polling lead averaging 7-8 points, though recent polls show tightening", "Prediction markets uniformly price Ossoff at 94%", "Republican-leaning pollsters still show Ossoff ahead despite favorable house effects"], "rationale": "The evidence strongly favors Ossoff: a 7-8 point polling average, a 10-rater expert consensus of Likely D, and 94% market pricing, backed by an overwhelming fundraising edge. I shade the baseline down slightly to 7 points because the most recent polls show some tightening and Collins has consolidated GOP support with Kemp's endorsement.", "watch": "If the tightening trend in late-September polls continues and national Republicans keep investing in Georgia, this could move toward Lean D territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Jon Ossoff (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Senator Jon Ossoff seeks a second term against Representative Mike Collins in a slightly Republican-leaning state. Ossoff has led in every public poll of the cycle, with recent surveys showing margins of four to nine points, and he holds an enormous financial advantage heading into the final weeks. Expert raters broadly rate the race Likely D, with a couple of Lean or Tilt D outliers.\n\nCollins, endorsed by Governor Kemp and backed by Trump, has consolidated the Republican side, and some recent polling suggests the race is tightening. Still, Ossoff's incumbency, fundraising dominance, and consistent polling lead make him a clear but not insurmountable favorite, with prediction markets pricing his win odds around 94 percent.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9499, "margin": 8.78}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8988, "margin": 7.0}]}